WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.599 --> 00:00:05.259
Robert Downey, Jr.'s first starring vehicle
since ending his tenure as Tony Stark - AKA

00:00:05.260 --> 00:00:09.480
Iron Man - in the Marvel Cinematic Universe
didn't quite set the box office on fire.

00:00:09.580 --> 00:00:13.940
In fact, Dolittle - a new take on that classic
childrens' character who talks to animals

00:00:13.940 --> 00:00:18.760
- is shaping up to be one of the biggest bombs
of 2020, and it's only January.

00:00:18.760 --> 00:00:23.680
Its opening four-day total came in at just
under $60 million worldwide, and while that's

00:00:23.690 --> 00:00:28.530
respectable for a January release, Dolittle
had to do much, much better to have a solid

00:00:28.530 --> 00:00:29.680
chance of breaking even.

00:00:29.890 --> 00:00:35.520
The movie was budgeted at an eye-watering
$175 million, and for reasons we'll get into

00:00:35.520 --> 00:00:39.660
momentarily, it likely ended up sporting a
much heftier price tag than that.

00:00:39.820 --> 00:00:44.539
On paper, the film looked like a winner: Downey
in the lead, an all-star cast including the

00:00:44.539 --> 00:00:49.940
likes of Rami Malek, Octavia Spencer, and
Tom Holland voicing all of those adorable

00:00:49.940 --> 00:00:53.569
animals, and an Oscar-winning writer/director
in Stephen Gaghan.

00:00:53.569 --> 00:00:57.140
So, why did Dolittle flop so spectacularly
at the box office?

00:00:57.420 --> 00:01:01.040
"We have no choice but to embark… on this
perilous journey."

00:01:01.320 --> 00:01:05.360
The first signs of trouble came when Dolittle,
after its initial test screenings, was found

00:01:05.489 --> 00:01:07.880
to be in need of heavy reshoots by Universal.

00:01:07.880 --> 00:01:11.880
In order to better connect with family and
overseas audiences, the studio decreed that

00:01:11.880 --> 00:01:15.960
the flick was in dire need of more talking
animals, more slapstick comedy, and - if the

00:01:15.960 --> 00:01:19.200
critics are to be believed - a whole heck
of a lot more fart jokes.

00:01:19.490 --> 00:01:23.720
"Something smelled wrong - and that's coming
from a guy who loves the smell of butts."

00:01:23.729 --> 00:01:26.869
For undisclosed reasons, Gaghan did not return
for these reshoots.

00:01:26.869 --> 00:01:31.619
Instead, the studio tapped Chris McKay and
Jonathan Liebesman to shoot the new footage,

00:01:31.620 --> 00:01:36.120
which almost certainly pushed the movie's
final budget well north of $200 million.

00:01:36.300 --> 00:01:39.960
But not only did the reshoots push the flick's
budget into the stratosphere, they delayed

00:01:39.969 --> 00:01:45.020
its release by nine months, and foreshadowed
a troubling fact: Universal wasn't quite sure

00:01:45.020 --> 00:01:46.590
who it was making this movie for.

00:01:46.590 --> 00:01:50.440
Now, don't get us wrong: Gaghan is a fine
writer and director, but the fact that he

00:01:50.440 --> 00:01:55.020
was hired for Dolittle only serves to illustrate
that this was a film conceived with no clear

00:01:55.020 --> 00:01:56.060
audience in mind.

00:01:56.120 --> 00:02:00.020
His previous credits didn't even approach
the realm of family-friendly; his stock in

00:02:00.020 --> 00:02:03.680
trade is serious dramas like Havoc, Traffic,
and Syriana.

00:02:04.000 --> 00:02:08.700
It was Gaghan's cut of the film that went
over like a lead balloon at those test screenings,

00:02:08.710 --> 00:02:12.950
and Universal's attempt to course-correct
by bringing in McKay and Liebesman to ramp

00:02:12.950 --> 00:02:15.240
up the silliness was obviously the wrong move.

00:02:15.380 --> 00:02:20.110
The film was all over the place in terms of
tone, described as a "grand adventure for

00:02:20.110 --> 00:02:22.900
the adults and fart and poop jokes for the
kids."

00:02:22.900 --> 00:02:25.860
Ultimately, it failed to win over either audience.

00:02:26.140 --> 00:02:30.280
To make matters worse, the film debuted in
January, a traditional dumping ground for

00:02:30.280 --> 00:02:33.200
flicks that studios know nobody is going to
want to see.

00:02:33.660 --> 00:02:37.911
This, coupled with its troubled production
history, caused critics to view it with suspicion

00:02:37.911 --> 00:02:42.640
before they even set foot in the theater - and
once they did, those suspicions were confirmed.

00:02:42.640 --> 00:02:47.310
Poor critical notices aren't always a determining
factor for tentpole films, but in regards

00:02:47.310 --> 00:02:51.340
to Dolittle, it's hard to make the case that
the film's reviews didn't punch some cannonball-sized

00:02:51.340 --> 00:02:52.560
holes in its hull.

00:02:52.860 --> 00:02:58.060
Critics weren't just unkind; they were absolutely
brutal, as summed up by the words of NPR's

00:02:58.060 --> 00:02:59.060
Scott Tobias.

00:02:59.500 --> 00:03:00.700
"Dolittle is not a film.

00:03:01.080 --> 00:03:04.040
Dolittle is a crime scene in need of forensic
analysis.

00:03:04.040 --> 00:03:05.100
Something happened here.

00:03:05.100 --> 00:03:06.040
Something terrible.

00:03:06.040 --> 00:03:07.460
Something inexplicable.

00:03:07.700 --> 00:03:12.150
[...] This one had been stabbed multiple times,
and only a thorough behind-the-scenes examination

00:03:12.150 --> 00:03:14.600
could sort out whose fingerprints are on what
hilt."

00:03:14.940 --> 00:03:21.140
To put things into perspective, consider that,
as of this writing, Dolittle stands at 19%

00:03:21.150 --> 00:03:25.910
on Rotten Tomatoes; the seemingly universally
reviled Cats, which is swiftly becoming synonymous

00:03:25.910 --> 00:03:30.100
with "unbelievably ill-conceived fiasco,"
sits at 20%.

00:03:30.410 --> 00:03:35.500
If Dolittle had opened to no competition whatsoever,
the damage might have at least been mitigated.

00:03:35.510 --> 00:03:39.400
But compounding the flick's myriad problems,
it had the distinct misfortune of opening

00:03:39.400 --> 00:03:44.040
against a franchise movie that had no trouble
generating word of mouth of a much more positive

00:03:44.040 --> 00:03:44.540
nature.

00:03:45.080 --> 00:03:46.420
Now it's time to be good men."

00:03:46.680 --> 00:03:49.480
"Who in the hell wanna sing that song?

00:03:49.480 --> 00:03:52.840
Good men, good men, what you gonna do?"

00:03:53.320 --> 00:03:57.560
Nobody ever accused the Will Smith/Martin
Lawrence buddy cop pictures Bad Boys and Bad

00:03:57.560 --> 00:04:02.360
Boys II of being high art, and it had been
nearly 17 years since the former flick's release.

00:04:02.400 --> 00:04:05.600
But Bad Boys for Life - which had been in
development for years, and which was made

00:04:05.600 --> 00:04:10.100
without the involvement of franchise director
Michael Bay - arrived to surprisingly solid

00:04:10.100 --> 00:04:15.010
reviews, and built a strong pre-release buzz
which resulted in it overperforming wildly.

00:04:15.010 --> 00:04:19.260
The modestly-budgeted flick has already reeled
in over $100 million [one-hundred-million-dollars,

00:04:19.260 --> 00:04:23.000
which is the second-best showing in history
for Martin Luther King Day weekend.

00:04:23.000 --> 00:04:27.360
The surprise smash all but sealed the box
office fortunes of Dolittle, which will now

00:04:27.360 --> 00:04:31.990
have to put up an unreasonably strong overseas
performance to even come within shouting distance

00:04:31.990 --> 00:04:32.980
of breaking even.

00:04:33.240 --> 00:04:37.860
It's a stunning reversal of fortune for Downey,
who went from starring in the highest-grossing

00:04:37.860 --> 00:04:43.180
film of all time - last year's Avengers: Endgame
- to a film that will be remembered as a towering

00:04:43.180 --> 00:04:45.140
flop, if it's remembered at all.

